\section{The G-MFRM Framework}
\label{sec:method}

Section~\ref{sec:confounding} established that omitting contamination from the measurement model produces directional bias in ability estimates (Observation~\ref{obs:ovb}). We now introduce the Gated Many-Facet Rasch Model (G-MFRM), which jointly estimates ability, judge severity, and contamination susceptibility within a single measurement framework. The key design choices, staged estimation with externally fixed exposure and a hierarchical HalfNormal prior for the contamination term, are motivated by the identifiability analysis in \S\ref{sec:identifiability} and the error propagation bounds in \S\ref{sec:error_prop}.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig1_framework}
\caption{Overview of the G-MFRM three-stage calibration procedure. Stage~1 fits a standard MFRM to obtain initial estimates $(\hat{\theta}, \hat{b}, \hat{d})$. Stage~2 estimates per-item exposure $e_i$ from external evidence (reference model anchoring and cross-model agreement). Stage~3 performs joint MCMC inference with $e_i$ fixed, recovering contamination-corrected ability $\tilde{\theta}_n$, contamination susceptibility $c_n$, and judge severity $d_j$.}
\label{fig:framework}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Model Specification}
\label{sec:model_spec}

G-MFRM extends the standard MFRM \citep{linacre1989mfrm, linacre1994mfrm} by adding a multiplicative contamination gating term to the linear predictor (Eq.~\ref{eq:mfrm}):
%
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:gmfrm}
\log \frac{P(Y_{nij} \geq k)}{P(Y_{nij} < k)} = \theta_n - b_i - d_j - \tau_k + c_n \cdot e_i,
\end{equation}
%
where all terms carry the definitions from Section~\ref{sec:confounding}. The gating structure $c_n \cdot e_i$ separates the model-specific and item-specific components of contamination: $c_n \geq 0$ captures how much model $n$ benefits from memorized benchmark content, while $e_i \in [0,1]$ quantifies how much of item $i$ has leaked into training corpora. When $c_n = 0$ (a clean model) or $e_i = 0$ (a novel item), the gating term vanishes and Eq.~\ref{eq:gmfrm} reduces to the standard MFRM. This graceful degradation ensures that G-MFRM imposes no distortion on uncontaminated model-item pairs.

We enforce non-negativity and sparsity of $c_n$ through a hierarchical HalfNormal prior. A global shrinkage scale $\sigma_c \sim \text{HalfNormal}(1.0)$ controls the overall contamination level, and per-model raw effects $c^{\mathrm{raw}}_n \sim \text{HalfNormal}(1.0)$ are scaled to obtain $c_n = c^{\mathrm{raw}}_n \cdot \sigma_c$. The HalfNormal support restricts $c_n \geq 0$, and the hierarchical structure provides adaptive shrinkage: when the data support low overall contamination, $\sigma_c$ contracts toward zero, pulling all $c_n$ values down. Models with genuine contamination retain large $c^{\mathrm{raw}}_n$ relative to the group. The remaining priors are $\theta_n \sim \mathcal{N}(0, 1)$, $b_i \sim \mathcal{N}(0, 1)$, $d_j \sim \mathcal{N}(0, 0.5)$, and standard uniform on thresholds $\tau_k$.

The closest antecedent is the Doubly-Gated IRT model of \citet{shu2013dgirt}, which introduced binary gating for item preknowledge in educational testing. Beyond extending DG-IRT to continuous exposure and multi-rater designs, G-MFRM's primary methodological contributions are analytical: the identifiability analysis (Propositions~\ref{prop:no_new}--\ref{prop:exposure_variation} and Theorem~\ref{thm:sufficient}) provides the first formal conditions under which gated IRT parameters are separable, and the Murphy--Topel error propagation framework (\S\ref{sec:error_prop}) quantifies how staged estimation uncertainty propagates to downstream parameter estimates---neither of which exists for DG-IRT or any prior gated measurement model.

\subsection{Staged Estimation}
\label{sec:staged}

Joint estimation of all parameters in Eq.~\ref{eq:gmfrm}, including $e_i$, would require identifying the bilinear product $c_n \cdot e_i$ from response data alone. This creates a fundamental non-identifiability (Proposition~\ref{prop:exposure_variation}). We resolve this through a three-stage procedure that fixes exposure externally before estimating the contamination parameters.

\paragraph{Stage 1: Standard MFRM.} We fit the standard MFRM (Eq.~\ref{eq:mfrm}) via MCMC (NUTS) to obtain initial estimates $(\hat{\theta}, \hat{b}, \hat{d})$. These serve as warm-start values for the sampler in Stage~3 and improve convergence.

\paragraph{Stage 2: Exposure estimation.} Per-item exposure $e_i$ is estimated from external evidence independent of the response data used in Stages~1 and~3. The primary signal is \emph{reference model anchoring}: using a fixed reference model (e.g., gpt-4-0314) as anchor, we compute win-rate residuals for each target model against difficulty-adjusted expectations; items where target models systematically outperform the anchor beyond what difficulty predicts are flagged as potentially exposed. The secondary signal is \emph{cross-model agreement}: items receiving uniformly high scores across architecturally diverse models suggest exposure rather than genuine easiness, quantified via $z$-score standardization of the inter-model agreement distribution. These signals are combined as a weighted composite (75\% anchoring, 25\% agreement), clipped to $[0, 1]$, and treated as fixed in Stage~3. Where model log-probabilities are accessible, LogProber can provide an additional gray-box memorization signal, though it is not used in our current experiments.

\paragraph{Non-circularity.} Exposure estimation is not circular: $e_i$ is item-level (content leakage) while $c_n$ is model-level (susceptibility), testing a cross-level interaction. The primary signal (75\%) derives from reference anchoring independent of target models, and ablation with 100\% anchoring yields $\rho = 0.998$ (Appendix~\ref{app:weight_robustness}), confirming $e_i$ is robust to removing the cross-model component.

\paragraph{Stage 3: Joint MCMC.} With $e_i$ fixed, we sample $(\theta_n, b_i, d_j, c_n)$ jointly via NUTS \citep{betancourt2015}. The priors are as specified in \S\ref{sec:model_spec}. The hierarchical contamination prior decomposes as
%
\begin{align}
\label{eq:contam_prior}
\sigma_c &\sim \text{HalfNormal}(1.0), \quad c^{\mathrm{raw}}_n \sim \text{HalfNormal}(1.0), \nonumber \\
c_n &= c^{\mathrm{raw}}_n \cdot \sigma_c,
\end{align}
%
where $\sigma_c$ is a shared global scale that governs the overall contamination magnitude. This non-centered parameterization improves sampler geometry by decoupling the global scale from the per-model effects.

Convergence is assessed via rank-normalized split-$\hat{R}$ \citep{vehtari2021rhat} and bulk effective sample size (ESS), with thresholds $\hat{R} < 1.05$ and ESS$_{\mathrm{bulk}} > 100$ following standard practice \citep{gelman2013bda}.\footnote{Synthetic validation with $N = 50$, $I = 100$, $J = 8$ yields satisfactory convergence across all parameters (exp-012). Full diagnostic tables are reported in Section~\ref{sec:experiments}.}


\subsection{Identifiability}
\label{sec:identifiability}

Adding a multiplicative term to a measurement model raises the question of whether the new parameters can be separated from the existing ones. We establish that fixing $e_i$ externally is both necessary and sufficient for identification. All proofs assume the standard location constraint $\sum_n \theta_n = 0$.

\begin{proposition}[No New Indeterminacy]
\label{prop:no_new}
When $e_i$ is fixed externally, the gating term $c_n \cdot e_i$ introduces no additional location or scale indeterminacy beyond that already present in the standard MFRM.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Suppose $c_n' = c_n + \delta$ preserves all response probabilities. Then $\delta \cdot e_i = 0$ for all items. Since $e_i \neq 0$ for at least one item by assumption, $\delta = 0$. The key observation is that fixing $e_i$ externally eliminates the bilinear ambiguity $c_n \cdot e_i = (\lambda c_n)(\lambda^{-1} e_i)$ that would otherwise render the decomposition non-unique.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Exposure Variation Necessary]
\label{prop:exposure_variation}
If $e_i = e$ for all $i$ (constant exposure), then $c_n$ and $\theta_n$ are not separately identifiable.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
When exposure is constant, the linear predictor becomes $(\theta_n + c_n \cdot e) - b_i - d_j - \tau_k$, which is equivalent to a standard MFRM with reparameterized ability $\theta^*_n = \theta_n + c_n \cdot e$. No configuration of response data can distinguish $(\theta_n, c_n)$ from $(\theta_n + \epsilon, c_n - \epsilon/e)$ for any $\epsilon$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
$\mathrm{Var}(e_i) > 0$ is a necessary condition for identifiability of $c_n$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Sufficient Conditions for Local Identifiability]
\label{thm:sufficient}
The G-MFRM is locally identifiable, up to the standard location constraint, if the following conditions hold: (i) a connected observation design (each item is observed by at least one model--judge pair), (ii) $J \geq 2$ judges, (iii) for each model $n$, the set of observed items spans at least two distinct exposure values ($\mathrm{Var}(e_i) > 0$ over observed items), and (iv) standard normalization $\sum_n \theta_n = 0$.
\end{theorem}

The proof, given in Appendix~\ref{app:proof}, follows \citet{rothenberg1971}: non-singularity of the Fisher information matrix is established via the Cauchy--Schwarz inequality on the $(\theta_n, c_n)$ submatrix determinant and a Schur complement argument for the remaining blocks. Conditions~(i) and~(ii) are standard multi-rater design requirements, satisfied by the connected design in our evaluation (44.7\% fill rate; \S\ref{sec:setup}); condition~(iii) is the substantive constraint that each model's observed items include varying exposure levels.

\subsection{Error Propagation}
\label{sec:error_prop}

The staged procedure treats the Stage~2 exposure estimates $\hat{e}_i$ as fixed quantities in Stage~3. This is a generated regressors problem \citep{pagan1984}: measurement error in $\hat{e}_i$ propagates into the posterior of $(\theta_n, c_n)$. The multiplicative structure $c_n \cdot e_i$ makes this an errors-in-variables (EIV) problem \citep{fuller1987}, with a predictable attenuation pattern.

Let $\hat{e}_i = e_i + \epsilon_i$ with $\epsilon_i \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2)$ independent of all model parameters. The attenuation factor for the contamination coefficient is
%
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:attenuation}
\lambda(\sigma) = \frac{\mathrm{Var}(e_i)}{\mathrm{Var}(e_i) + \sigma^2},
\end{equation}
%
so attenuation reaches 50\% when $\sigma = \sqrt{\mathrm{Var}(e_i)}$. In the synthetic DGP ($\mathrm{Var}(e_i) \approx 0.04$), this corresponds to $\sigma \approx 0.2$.\footnote{The attenuation formula assumes that $\epsilon_i$ is independent of all model parameters, identically distributed, and Gaussian. In practice, exposure estimation errors may be correlated across items sharing the same source or heteroscedastic across difficulty levels. Table~\ref{tab:error_prop} provides empirical validation that $\hat{\theta}$ estimates remain robust under such departures (Section~\ref{sec:experiments}).}

Table~\ref{tab:error_prop} shows the empirical degradation across noise levels (see also Figure~\ref{fig:error_prop} in the Appendix). Below the critical threshold ($\sigma \leq 0.2$), degradation is mild: $\theta$-RMSE increases by 5.9\% (from $0.128 \pm 0.018$ to $0.135 \pm 0.018$) and $c_n$-RMSE by 7.9\% (from $0.533 \pm 0.187$ to $0.575 \pm 0.198$). At $\sigma = 0.1$, the increases are only 1.4\% and 2.3\% respectively. Beyond the threshold ($\sigma = 0.5$), degradation becomes severe: $\theta$-RMSE rises 36.9\% and $c_n$-RMSE rises 57.9\%. Contamination estimates $c_n$ are more sensitive than ability estimates $\theta_n$ because $c_n$ enters the likelihood only through its product with $e_i$ (multiplicative coupling), while $\theta_n$ contributes additively and is anchored by all observations regardless of exposure.

These bounds make the staged approximation falsifiable: if the noise level of the Stage~2 exposure estimates can be bounded below $\sigma_{\mathrm{crit}}$, the resulting ability estimates carry quantifiable error guarantees. For honest credible intervals that account for generated-regressor uncertainty, we apply the Murphy-Topel variance correction \citep{murphy1985} to inflate the Stage~3 posterior variance by the estimated Stage~2 sampling variability.

\subsection{Fair Scores and Diagnostics}
\label{sec:fair_scores}

The Stage~3 posterior mean of $\theta_n$ provides a bias-and-contamination-corrected ``fair score'' for each model. The remaining parameters serve complementary diagnostic roles: per-model $c_n$ flags candidates for training data audit, per-judge $d_j$ informs judge calibration, and per-item $e_i$ identifies items for retirement or replacement.

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

\paragraph{Data and models.} We evaluate on Arena-Hard \citep{li2024arenahard}, a benchmark of 316 multi-turn conversational items selected for high separability among frontier models. After filtering for coverage, the evaluation set comprises 176 items in a connected incomplete design (XX{,}XXX observations; XX.X\% fill rate of the full $N \times I \times J$ tensor). Seventeen target models spanning proprietary and open-source families are evaluated: the original 8 models (GPT-4o, GPT-4o-mini, Qwen2.5-72B, LLaMA-3.3-70B, Qwen3-8B, Gemma-2-9B, GPT-4-0613, GPT-3.5-Turbo) plus 9 additional models (Claude-3.5-Sonnet, GPT-4-Turbo, LLaMA-3.1-70B, Gemma-2-27B, Claude-3-Opus, LLaMA-3-70B, Mixtral-8x22B, Claude-3-Haiku, LLaMA-3-8B). Ground-truth rankings are derived from Chatbot Arena human preference data \citep{zheng2023mtbench}.

\paragraph{Judges.} Nine LLM judges score model responses in position-swapped pairwise mode: GPT-5.5, Claude Opus 4.7, Claude Sonnet 4.6, DeepSeek-V4-Pro, Gemini-2.5-Pro, Mistral-Large-2, LLaMA-3.3-70B, Qwen2.5-72B, and Qwen3-235B. Seven judges cover all 17 target models; two judges (GPT-5.5 and Claude Opus 4.7) cover only the original 8 models, yielding an asymmetric connected design---Theorem~\ref{thm:sufficient} requires only connectivity, not full crossing. Position swapping controls for position bias and doubles the effective observation count. Two models---Qwen2.5-72B and LLaMA-3.3-70B---serve as both judge and target; $d_j$ absorbs systematic self-evaluation bias, and excluding self-judging observations leaves rankings unchanged ($\tau =$ TBD; Appendix~\ref{app:self_eval}).

\paragraph{Exposure estimation.} Per-item exposure $e_i$ is estimated via a two-source pipeline (\S\ref{sec:staged}): reference model anchoring with gpt-4-0314 as anchor (75\% weight) and cross-model agreement (25\% weight). The pipeline is robust to weight configuration (Spearman $\rho \geq 0.998$ across all configurations tested).

\paragraph{MCMC.} We use NumPyro NUTS with 4{,}000 warmup and 4{,}000 sampling iterations across 4 chains (target acceptance 0.95). The contamination prior follows the hierarchical HalfNormal specification (Eq.~\ref{eq:contam_prior}). Convergence is assessed via rank-normalized split-$\hat{R}$ \citep{vehtari2021rhat} with conventional $\hat{R} < 1.05$ and ESS $> 100$ thresholds \citep{gelman2013bda}.

\subsection{Baselines and Main Results}
\label{sec:main_results}

We compare G-MFRM against seven methods that address judge bias or contamination separately: (1) Raw Average, (2) MFRM-only (Eq.~\ref{eq:mfrm}), (3) Sequential pipeline (MFRM then contamination adjustment), (4) CyclicJudge \citep{cyclicjudge2026}, (5) CyclicJudge+discard (exposed items removed), (6) DCR Factor \citep{dcr2025}, and (7) Preference Leakage calibration \citep{preference_leakage2026}. All methods are evaluated by Kendall $\tau$ and Spearman $\rho$ against Chatbot Arena human rankings.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
& \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{8 models}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{17 models}} \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-3} \cmidrule(lr){4-5}
\textbf{Method} & $\tau$ & $\rho$ & $\tau$ & $\rho$ \\
\midrule
Raw Average & 0.786 & 0.905 & TBD & TBD \\
MFRM-only & 0.786 & 0.905 & TBD & TBD \\
