\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{Judge Bias in LLM Evaluation.}
CyclicJudge \citep{cyclicjudge2026} eliminates average bias via round-robin assignment but treats residual variation as genuine ability. UDA \citep{uda2026} learns a neural K-factor reducing inter-judge deviation by 63.4\% without modeling contamination. \citet{fiedler2026bias_uncertainty} formalize judge variability via $J/\Delta J$ diagnostics; \citet{ye2024justice} and \citet{panickssery2024selffavor} document position, verbosity, and self-favoritism biases. All model only the judge facet, absorbing contamination into abilities (Section~\ref{sec:confounding}).

\paragraph{Contamination Detection and Avoidance.}
ConStat \citep{constat2024}, DCR \citep{dcr2025}, LogProber \citep{logprober2024}, and Min-K\% \citep{mink2024} detect contamination but assume a fixed rater; \citet{preference_leakage2026} identify judge-side leakage, complementary to G-MFRM's examinee-side focus. LiveBench \citep{livebench2025}, TRUCE \citep{truce2024}, and tinyBenchmarks \citep{polo2024tinybenchmarks} prevent contamination by design; G-MFRM corrects for residual contamination in existing benchmarks.

\paragraph{IRT and MFRM for LLM Evaluation.}
\citet{jiao2025mfrm_llm} apply MFRM to LLM raters; \citet{uto2020generalized}, \citet{choi2026irt_judge}, and PSN-IRT \citep{zhou2025psnirt} extend IRT/MFRM but none include contamination facets. DG-IRT \citep{shu2013dgirt} is compared in Section~\ref{sec:model_spec}.

